{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The argument collapses the distinction between 'availability' and 'preferability.' Popper's problem asks why scientists *prefer* (act upon) the better-corroborated theory when multiple unfalsified theories exist, not why they choose from the set of survivors. In many scientific contexts, there is more than one 'last man standing' (multiple unfalsified competitors). If the preference were merely tautological based on survival, there would be no basis to choose Theory A over Theory B if both have survived equally well so far. The target's claim that preference is 'merely the absence of a reason to reject' fails to explain the *positive* weighting given to severity of tests (corroboration) over mere survival time or quantity of tests. By reducing the preference to a tautology of existence ('only ones left'), the target evades the core dilemma: acting on a theory implies an expectation of its future reliability, which looks suspiciously like the induction Popper rejected. If the preference is truly non-inductive, it cannot justify action in high-stakes scenarios where one must bet on a specific outcome among several unfalsified options; if it does justify action, it smuggles in the very inductive assumption (that past survival predicts future success) that the 'evolutionary' analogy was meant to bypass without admitting.","counterexample":["Consider two unfalsified theories, T1 (severely tested, highly corroborated) and T2 (barely tested, not yet falsified). Both are 'survivors.' The target's logic suggests preference is just 'absence of rejection,' implying T1 and T2 are equally preferable since neither is dead. Yet Popper and scientists strictly prefer T1. This preference cannot be explained by 'tautological survival' alone, requiring an additional principle that links severe testing to reliability—a principle that risks reintroducing induction."],"premise":"The target claims the preference for corroborated theories is defensible as a 'tautological result' of selection because they are 'the only ones left to choose,' thereby denying any inductive inference about the future.","successor_question":"If the preference is not inductive, what rational basis distinguishes acting on a severely tested survivor versus an untested survivor when both are currently unfalsified?","target":"e32b35c538d724a73e26857702bb771e8a2939c735d0ecc1a9327a7ab5df157b"}]}